<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of the Archipelago</h1><p class="st-meta">1207 CE – 1579 CE · capital Naxos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Latin duchy of the Cyclades centred on Naxos, founded by the Venetian Marco Sanudo, which outlived most crusader states in Greece by more than a century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1207 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-sanudo">Marco Sanudo</a>, nephew of the doge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enrico-dandolo">Enrico Dandolo</a>, led a private Venetian expedition that conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/naxos">Naxos</a> and other Cycladic islands, sharing some with fellow adventurers. He took the title Duke of the Archipelago under the suzerainty of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a> and later of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dukes ruled from the Kastro of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/naxos">Naxos</a>, granting fiefs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> lords on islands such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/andros">Andros</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/paros">Paros</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/melos">Milos</a>. A Catholic minority lived alongside a Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> majority, which kept its church. Revenues came from agriculture, emery and trade. In 1383 Francesco I <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crispo-family">Crispo</a> seized the duchy and founded a new dynasty.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayreddin-barbarossa">Barbarossa</a>'s raids of 1537 made the duchy an Ottoman tributary. In 1566 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giacomo-iv-crispo">Giacomo IV Crispo</a> and gave the duchy to the Jewish financier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-nasi">Joseph Nasi</a>, who ruled through a deputy. After his death in 1579 it was annexed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-the-archipelago.jpg" alt="Duchy of the Archipelago" width="320" height="352"><figcaption>Image: VIGNERON · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crispi_Coat_of_Arms.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1207 CE – 1579 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Naxos</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Marco Sanudo, Francesco I Crispo, Joseph Nasi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Eastern Orthodox Christianity (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian, Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Naxos, Duchy of the Aegean</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1393&amp;focus=duchy-of-the-archipelago" aria-label="Duchy of the Archipelago on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5316 841 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of the Archipelago lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5636 1097L5709 970M5636 1097L5530 1071M5636 1097L5293 831M5636 1097L5709 970M5636 1097L5629 1083M5636 1097L5622 1073"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5530 1071h0M5293 831h0M5709 970h0M5629 1083h0M5622 1073h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5636 1097h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5636 1097h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Naxos. <a href="/atlas?year=1393&amp;focus=duchy-of-the-archipelago">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

